{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792666996,"nonce":"HFtHa3Xg6wGcp+nynaBNFlstSXM=","title":"CodeEditor: zone widgets, an element placed between two lines that takes its own height","message":"Split from ed51114 (item 2), which the code diff view did not need: its rows are all one height.\n\n**What is wanted**\n\nAn element placed between two lines of a CodeEditor, taking its own height and scrolling with the text. Callers give a line number and an element; the zone moves with the text as gutter markers do (the follow option of 495aa31). Uses: a peek diff under a changed line, 'Accept current | Accept incoming' above a conflict, inline blame.\n\n**Where it bites**\n\nCodeEditor assumes one row height everywhere: row top, caret, rectAt and offsetAt, contentHeight, the window of rows, the minimap, TextEditing's lineAtY. A zone adds a y offset to every row after it, so each of those needs a line-to-y map in place of PAD + row * lineHeight. Settle that map first, then the zone.","files":null}]}